  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on simulated future scenarios and simplified carbon storage coefficients in the InVEST model, which may not fully capture real-world microclimate and soil carbon dynamics.",
      "what": "This study simulated land use and carbon storage dynamics in the Chengdu–Chongqing region of China from 2005 to 2020 and projected future changes under SSP126, SSP245, and SSP585 scenarios using the PLUS and InVEST models. The historical analysis revealed that built-up land expansion at the expense of cropland drove carbon losses, while grassland-to-forest transitions partially offset this by concentrating carbon storage in forested areas. Future projections indicate that carbon storage peaks under the SSP126 scenario, driven by an 11.97% expansion in forest cover.",
      "why": "It quantifies the specific carbon trade-offs between urban expansion and ecological restoration in a rapidly growing Chinese urban agglomeration, demonstrating that targeted forest restoration under a low-emission pathway can maximize regional carbon sequestration."
    }
  },
